    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Edwin of Northumbria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_King_Edwin_of_Northumbria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DaveWebster14, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercia, from the Old English <em>Mierce</em>, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper Trent valley, around what later became Staffordshire, Derbyshire and Leicestershire. Its beginnings are obscure. Later royal genealogies trace the kings back to Icel and name Creoda and his son Pybba as early rulers in the late 6th century, and the traditional starting date of 527 rests on such late sources rather than on contemporary record. The first Mercian king named by Bede is Cearl, whose daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin of Northumbria</a> in the early 7th century. Mercia emerged as a real power under Penda (d. 655), a pagan warlord who probably became king in the 620s or 630s. In alliance with the British king Cadwallon of Gwynedd he defeated and killed Edwin at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633, and in 642 he killed Oswald of Northumbria at Maserfield. Penda also killed several kings of East Anglia and set his son Peada over the Middle Angles. By absorbing smaller peoples of the Midlands, such as the Middle Angles, the Hwicce around Worcester and the Magonsaete on the Welsh border, Mercia grew from a frontier people into the largest of the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#london"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="London" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Skyline_(125508655).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ilya Grigorik, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercia was a land of mixed farming in the river valleys of the Trent, Severn and Avon, organised around royal estates that collected food renders from the surrounding countryside. A document known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tribal-hidage">Tribal Hidage</a>, probably drawn up for a Mercian overlord in the 7th or 8th century, assesses the peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> in hides, the units of land that supported a household and owed dues to the king. Society was led by the king and a warrior aristocracy bound to him by gifts of land and treasure; beneath them were free farmers and a large unfree population. The Staffordshire Hoard, found near Lichfield in 2009, contains several thousand pieces of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> war gear from the 7th century and shows the wealth that circulated among Mercian warriors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> arrived after Penda's death through Irish-trained missionaries from Northumbria; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chad">Chad</a> established the see of Lichfield in 669, and monasteries such as Repton, Medeshamstede (Peterborough) and Breedon flourished. Control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a>, the leading port of southern England, gave the kings access to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> a broad, carefully designed silver penny became the standard coin of much of England.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Offa of Mercia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Offa,_King_of_Mercia,_silver_penny;_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Offa (07..-0796 ; roi de Mercie). Autorité émettrice de monnaie, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian warfare was the warfare of a royal war band, the king's sworn companions reinforced by levies from client peoples and allies. Penda's victories at Hatfield Chase in 633 and Maserfield in 642 made Mercia the dominant power in the Midlands, but his defeat and death at the hands of Oswiu of Northumbria at the Battle of the Winwaed in 655 led to three years of Northumbrian occupation. A rising of Mercian nobles in 658 placed Penda's son Wulfhere on the throne, and his brother Æthelred defeated Ecgfrith of Northumbria at the Battle of the Trent in 679, ending Northumbrian claims south of the Humber and recovering Lindsey. In the 8th century Æthelbald and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> fought the Welsh kingdoms and compelled the southern kingdoms to accept their lordship. Offa's Dyke, an earthwork bank and ditch running for much of the Welsh border, was attributed to Offa by Asser in the 890s and is the largest earthwork of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>. Mercian kings also imposed obligations of bridge and fortress building on their lands. The army was broken by Ecgberht of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wessex">Wessex</a> at the Battle of Ellendun in 825 and could not withstand the Great Heathen Army, which occupied Repton in 873–874.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#papacy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papacy.jpg" alt="Papacy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#papacy">Papacy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Leo_XIV_3_(3x4_cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edgar Beltrán, The Pillar, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian kings ruled through itinerant courts that moved between royal estates such as Tamworth, their principal residence, and the church centres of Lichfield and Repton, where several kings were buried. Royal succession was open to any male of royal descent, and in the 8th and 9th centuries the throne passed between rival kindreds, often violently: Æthelbald was murdered by his own bodyguard at Seckington in 757. Mercian power rested on overlordship. Client kings of the Hwicce, the Magonsaete and Lindsey were gradually reduced to ealdormen, and kings of Kent, Sussex and East Anglia either submitted or were removed. Æthelbald was styled 'king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>' in a charter of 736. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> (757–796) went further, annexing Kent and Sussex, having Æthelberht II of East Anglia beheaded in 794 and persuading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a> to create an archbishopric at Lichfield in 787, which lasted until 803. In 787 he also had his son Ecgfrith consecrated as king, the first such anointing recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>. Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> and nobles, often held at a place called Clofesho, issued charters and settled church affairs. Offa's legislation is mentioned by Alfred, though its text does not survive.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of the 8th century Mercia was the leading kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, and historians speak of a 'Mercian supremacy' under Æthelbald and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>. Offa's correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, including a letter of 796 concerning trade and the protection of merchants and pilgrims, placed an English king in diplomatic exchange with the greatest ruler of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> penny set a pattern of coinage that English kings followed for centuries, and a gold coin imitating an Abbasid dinar with the added words OFFA REX shows the reach of contemporary trade. Mercian churches and scriptoria produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> and sculpture of high quality, including the carvings at Breedon on the Hill and the church at Brixworth, and Mercian scholars mattered to the later English revival: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> recruited four Mercian clerics, among them Wærferth of Worcester and Plegmund, later archbishop of Canterbury, to help with his programme of learning. The Mercian dialect of Old English shaped later written English. The name survived the kingdom in the great Earldom of Mercia of the 11th century, held by Leofric and his family, and Offa's Dyke remains one of the best-known monuments of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alfred-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great.jpg" alt="Alfred the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_the_Great,_silver_penny;_struck_875%E2%80%93880_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG - Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mercian dominance collapsed within a generation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>'s death. Coenwulf (796–821) held much of it together and crushed a revolt in Kent in 798, but disputed successions followed, and in 825 Ecgberht of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wessex">Wessex</a> defeated King Beornwulf at the Battle of Ellendun. Wessex took over Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex, East Anglia broke free, and in 829 Ecgberht briefly ruled Mercia itself before Wiglaf recovered it in 830. In the following decades Mercia cooperated with Wessex, and King Burgred married Æthelswith, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> Great Heathen Army wintered at Nottingham in 867–868 and at Repton in 873–874, forcing Burgred to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>. The Vikings installed Ceolwulf II and in 877 divided the kingdom, settling the eastern half, later part of the Danelaw, and leaving the west to him. The western half was then ruled by Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians, who accepted Alfred's overlordship and married Alfred's daughter Æthelflæd. After his death in 911 Æthelflæd ruled alone, built a chain of fortified burhs and recovered Derby and Leicester. When she died in June 918, her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> deposed her daughter Ælfwynn and took Mercia into the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mercia, the midland kingdom of Penda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>, was the dominant power of 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, issuing the penny that became England's standard coin, raising Offa's Dyke and dealing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wessex">Wessex</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Vikings</a> divided it and Æthelflæd's western Mercia merged into the emerging English kingdom.</em></p>

